WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:11.736
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:11.736 --> 00:00:15.790
PRASHANTH VENKATARAM: By about
high school, I knew that I

00:00:15.790 --> 00:00:17.440
wanted to do something
in physics.

00:00:19.980 --> 00:00:25.205
I knew that MIT would be
a great place for that.

00:00:25.205 --> 00:00:31.345
And I had heard about how
undergrads could do research.

00:00:34.464 --> 00:00:36.170
ALEJANDRO RODRIGUEZ: Prashanth
is extremely

00:00:36.170 --> 00:00:39.480
smart, extremely motivated.

00:00:39.480 --> 00:00:41.320
One of the key things
undergraduates bring to the

00:00:41.320 --> 00:00:45.370
table is a fresh perspective,
a willingness to essentially

00:00:45.370 --> 00:00:50.470
try things that sometimes, a
sort of a seasoned researcher,

00:00:50.470 --> 00:00:51.860
you sort of overlook.

00:00:51.860 --> 00:00:53.330
You think you know what the
answer's going to be.

00:00:53.330 --> 00:00:55.910
You let hubris essentially
take over.

00:00:55.910 --> 00:00:57.455
And you don't explore things
that sometimes

00:00:57.455 --> 00:00:59.070
undergraduates do.

00:00:59.070 --> 00:01:00.970
And sometimes, they
hit the jackpot.

00:01:00.970 --> 00:01:01.670
So what is this?

00:01:01.670 --> 00:01:02.700
What are we looking at?

00:01:02.700 --> 00:01:04.560
PRASHANTH VENKATARAM: These are
the plots of the average

00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:07.690
flux with respect
to frequency.

00:01:07.690 --> 00:01:10.590
And just for your convenience,
I've shown--

00:01:10.590 --> 00:01:14.930
What I've been doing is to model
one particular type of a

00:01:14.930 --> 00:01:19.360
new class of structures called
photonic crystals.

00:01:19.360 --> 00:01:20.750
ALEJANDRO RODRIGUEZ: What
is a photonic crystal?

00:01:20.750 --> 00:01:24.120
Well, it's a periodic structure,
with features where

00:01:24.120 --> 00:01:26.200
the periodicity of the
structure is on the

00:01:26.200 --> 00:01:28.140
wavelength of light.

00:01:28.140 --> 00:01:32.050
PRASHANTH VENKATARAM: I'm
studying what frequencies and

00:01:32.050 --> 00:01:37.050
wavelengths of light are emitted
the most when parts of

00:01:37.050 --> 00:01:38.910
the crystal are excited.

00:01:38.910 --> 00:01:44.970
Here, there are two very
distinct peaks where the

00:01:44.970 --> 00:01:49.570
energy emitted is much higher
for those ranges of frequency.

00:01:49.570 --> 00:01:51.360
ALEJANDRO RODRIGUEZ: The
importance of that work is

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:54.310
that a lot of these fluorescent
molecules are

00:01:54.310 --> 00:02:00.420
being used to do spectroscopy
or to monitor cells or

00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:02.650
microscopic and nanoscopic
objects.

00:02:02.650 --> 00:02:05.540
Yeah, this is essentially
your unit cell

00:02:05.540 --> 00:02:07.040
size, you know, lambda.

00:02:07.040 --> 00:02:09.960
So for example, one might
think of essentially

00:02:09.960 --> 00:02:13.460
piggybacking one of these
molecules in a tumor cell and

00:02:13.460 --> 00:02:15.860
imaging, essentially, the
behavior of the tumor cell as

00:02:15.860 --> 00:02:18.415
a function of time or as
a function of space.

00:02:18.415 --> 00:02:19.940
PRASHANTH VENKATARAM: I mean,
if it's Lawrencian, it seems

00:02:19.940 --> 00:02:20.845
fairly straightforward, right?

00:02:20.845 --> 00:02:22.036
ALEJANDRO RODRIGUEZ:
Yeah, yeah.

00:02:22.036 --> 00:02:24.760
PRASHANTH VENKATARAM: Research
is not limited to postdocs and

00:02:24.760 --> 00:02:26.260
grad students.

00:02:26.260 --> 00:02:28.940
Undergrads can do it, too.

00:02:28.940 --> 00:02:33.180
And often, they contribute a
very vital role in some of the

00:02:33.180 --> 00:02:34.470
research that goes on.

00:02:34.470 --> 00:02:37.790
ALEJANDRO RODRIGUEZ: And this
is basically tangentially--

00:02:37.790 --> 00:02:40.320
I was an undergraduate here.

00:02:40.320 --> 00:02:42.710
One of the reasons I came to
MIT was actually because of

00:02:42.710 --> 00:02:44.000
the UROP program.

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:46.170
Once you find what you're really
interested in or what

00:02:46.170 --> 00:02:48.830
you want to do, you can
continue doing that.

00:02:48.830 --> 00:02:50.690
And that can eventually
develop into

00:02:50.690 --> 00:02:52.810
your graduate program.

00:02:52.810 --> 00:02:55.030
I wouldn't be where I am today,
actually, if it weren't

00:02:55.030 --> 00:02:55.630
for the UROP program.

00:02:55.630 --> 00:03:01.625
[MUSIC PLAYING]

